---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Sprout Siege · Lab · Brett Boggs"
  description="An idle farm game for iOS, built around a simulation core that never touches the screen."
>
  <article>
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Lab · Aug 2026</p>
      <h1>Sprout Siege</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        An idle farm game for iOS, built around a simulation core that never touches the screen.
      </p>
    </header>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img src="/sprout-siege/shot-farm.webp" width="1600" height="782"
           alt="The farm: low poly plots, windmills and a barn, with collect, new plot and defend across the bottom." />
      <figcaption>The farm. It runs in real time, and nothing is lost while you are away.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <dl class="figures">
      <div>
        <dt>115</dt>
        <dd>Swift files across a package and a thin app target</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>268</dt>
        <dd>tests in 29 suites, running headless</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>v7</dt>
        <dd>save format, every version with a migration path</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>One rule</h2>
      <p>
        The package holding the game cannot import a user interface framework. No SwiftUI, no
        SpriteKit, no UIKit. Economy, waves, upgrades, prestige and saves all live in there and run
        headless under <code>swift test</code>. The app target stays thin: menus and HUD in
        SwiftUI, the combat board in SpriteKit, and no game rules in either. If a change needs the
        core to know about the screen, the change is wrong.
      </p>

      <figure class="shot">
        <img src="/sprout-siege/shot-defense.webp" width="1600" height="782"
             alt="The defense board: a winding lane across a field, four defenders placed, and the defender tray with prices." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>SpriteKit draws the board. The package decides what happens on it.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Deterministic by construction</h2>
      <p>
        The simulation is tick based rather than frame based. It advances by an interval, and every
        random number comes from an injected seeded generator, so the same seed and the same inputs
        produce the same result every time. Offline progress is not a second code path. Coming back
        after two days is the same call with a larger number. Balance lives in JSON rather than
        Swift, so tuning the game never requires touching code.
      </p>

      <h2>What that caught</h2>
      <p>
        Partway in, the structure surfaced a design problem I had not seen. Offline income was
        being generated by tower defense waves fighting themselves in an empty room. The player
        came back to a battle they had made no decisions in, which is neither believable nor good
        idle design.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fix was structural. The farm became the idle spine: it runs in real time, and nothing
        wilts or expires or is ever lost while you sleep. Tower defense moved to being the first
        side game, short, active, and something you have to be present for, where you can lose what
        you were carrying. Two layers instead of one, with the passive half where passive actually
        makes sense.
      </p>

      <figure class="shot">
        <img src="/sprout-siege/shot-risk.webp" width="1600" height="782"
             alt="The stake screen: four risk levels from Steady to Harvest Gamble, each with a payout multiplier and what it costs to lose." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>You pick what you are risking before the run starts.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="colophon">Swift 6, iOS 17 and up. Building clean, running in the simulator.</p>
    </div>

    <a class="back-link" href="/lab/">Back to Lab</a>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .shot {
    margin: 3rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .prose .shot {
    margin: 2.2rem 0;
  }
  .shot img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .shot figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.7rem;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  /* figures as editorial rows, matching the index. no tiles. */
  .figures {
    margin: 3rem 0 0;
  }
  .figures > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(5rem, 7rem) 1fr;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.4rem 1.6rem;
    padding: 1.3rem 0;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .figures dt {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 90, 'SOFT' 50, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .figures dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .prose code {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.92em;
    background: var(--paper-deep);
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .figures > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
